questions.starter_code:
const orders = [
  { id: 1, status: "paid" },
  { id: 2, status: "pending" },
  { id: 3, status: "paid" },
];

const groupByStatus = (list) => {
  // your code
};

console.log(groupByStatus(orders));

questions.answer:
const groupByStatus = (list) => {
  const groups = list.reduce((acc, order) => {
    (acc[order.status] ??= []).push(order.id);
    return acc;
  }, {});

  return Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(groups).sort(([a], [b]) => a.localeCompare(b))
  );
};

console.log(groupByStatus(orders));
// { paid: [ 1, 3 ], pending: [ 2 ] }

The accumulator must be returned on every iteration, otherwise the next step receives undefined. acc[key] ??= [] creates the bucket only when missing (logical assignment, ES2021), then push adds the item. Sorting Object.entries gives alphabetical keys, and Object.fromEntries turns the pairs back into an object. If order of keys didn't matter, you could return groups directly.

